                            Preamble

  The GNU General Public License is a free, copyleft license for
software and other kinds of works.

  The licenses for most software and other practical works are designed
to take away your freedom to share and change the works.  By contrast,
the GNU General Public License is intended to guarantee your freedom to
share and change all versions of a program--to make sure it remains free
software for all its users.  We, the Free Software Foundation, use the
GNU General Public License for most of our software; it is also made
available to the public for any program that wishes to use it.

  When we speak of free software, we are referring to freedom, not
price.  Our General Public Licenses are designed to make sure that you
have the freedom to distribute copies of free software (and charge for
them if you wish), that you have the freedom to receive free software,
that you have the freedom to modify the software or to use parts of it
in new free programs, and that you have the freedom to know or can
obtain the source code.  If you do not have the freedom to obtain the
source code, if the source code is kept secret, or if the source code
is not available to the public, the license provides for ways to help
you obtain it.

  For developers' work, the GNU General Public License protects your
rights by requiring that anyone who distributes copies of the software,
with or without modifications, gives the recipients the same freedoms
that you have.  By forcing anyone who distributes the software to
pass on the freedoms you receive, we can guarantee that the software
and its freedoms are preserved for all its users.

  The GNU General Public License does not require you to give up any
copyright; it requires you to distribute the software with the same
terms, so that the freedoms you receive are preserved for all users.

  In order to protect your rights, we need to prevent others from
denying you these rights or forcing you to surrender your rights.
Therefore, you must pass on the freedoms to the recipients.

  To ensure this, the GNU General Public License requires that
modified versions of the software must be marked as changed so that
their previous terms are not lost.  It requires that anyone who
distributes the software must give the recipients the same freedoms
that they received from you.  That is why the GNU General Public License
is sometimes called a "copyleft" license.

  Precise text of the GPL-3.0 license is available at:
  https://www.gnu.org/licenses/gpl-3.0.txt

